# Database and typed SQL

Odroe provides a small typed relational query layer over explicit database
drivers. It is intentionally not a full identity-map ORM and it does not hide
migrations, connection scope, or provider differences.

## One shared transport

```dart
abstract interface class SqlExecutor {
  Future<List<T>> query<T>(
    BoundSql statement,
    T Function(SqlRow row) decode,
  );

  Future<SqlWriteResult> execute(BoundSql statement);
}

abstract interface class SqlDatabase implements SqlExecutor {
  Future<List<SqlWriteResult>> atomicWrite(List<BoundSql> statements);
  Future<void> close();
}
```

`TransactionalSqlDatabase` is a separate capability. D1 can support atomic
batches without pretending to support an interactive transaction callback.

## Types stop mistakes before I/O

A `SqlTable<T>` owns columns and a projection. `SqlQueries` creates select,
insert, update, and delete statements for an explicit dialect:

```dart
final posts = Posts();
const queries = SqlQueries(SqlDialect.sqlite);

final post = await queries
    .selectTable(posts, where: posts.id.equals(42))
    .oneOrNull(database);

final totalPosts = await queries.countRows(posts).one(database);
```

Use `all` for collections. `oneOrNull` returns `null` for no row and rejects
multiple rows, preserving an exact zero-or-one cardinality contract. The table
projection can decode a complete application record; the caller does not need
to rebuild an entity from unrelated scalar queries.

`countRows` compiles typed `COUNT(*)` through the same table, join, and
predicate scope. It counts relation rows, so one-to-many joins can increase the
result; an empty relation returns `0`. Ordering, pagination, grouping, and a
general aggregate DSL are not part of this focused operation.

## Membership stays typed

Batch reads and filters remain on the same typed predicate path:

```dart
final selected = await queries
    .selectTable(
      posts,
      where: posts.id.isIn(<int>[42, 44]),
      orderBy: <SqlOrder>[posts.id.ascending],
    )
    .all(database);
```

`isIn` and `isNotIn` consume the iterable immediately and encode every non-null
candidate through the column codec. Adding, removing, or reordering source
collection entries later cannot change the predicate; copying an element's
mutable payload remains the codec's contract. Non-null candidate order and
duplicates are retained; Odroe does not auto-deduplicate, chunk, or bypass a
provider's bound-parameter limit.

Empty collections are safe and explicit: `isIn([])` never matches, while
`isNotIn([])` throws `ArgumentError`. A dynamic exclusion cannot silently turn
an UPDATE or DELETE into a full-table mutation; intentional full-table writes
keep using `updateAll` or `deleteAll` with `allRows`.

An explicit Dart `null` candidate becomes an `IS NULL` or `IS NOT NULL` branch
instead of entering the membership list. A custom codec remains authoritative
for every non-null Dart candidate and may encode one as SQL `NULL`. Without a
Dart null candidate, a nullable database value keeps SQL's three-valued
behavior and matches neither `IN` nor `NOT IN`.

Idempotent inserts keep the conflict key visible:

```dart
await queries
    .insertOnConflictDoNothing(
      posts,
      <SqlAssignment>[
        posts.id.set(42),
        posts.title.set('First post'),
      ],
      target: [posts.id],
    )
    .execute(database);
```

The column list must match a conflict arbiter accepted by the selected
database. Other index shapes remain explicit `BoundSql`. SQLite, D1, and
PostgreSQL support this exact target-aware operation. MySQL is rejected before
Odroe reads the inputs or performs database I/O because its conflict operations
do not preserve this contract.

## One statement for many rows

Use `insertMany` when rows share one typed column shape and should be sent as a
single multi-row `INSERT`:

```dart
final inserted = await queries
    .insertMany(posts, <List<SqlAssignment>>[
      <SqlAssignment>[posts.id.set(43), posts.title.set('First')],
      <SqlAssignment>[posts.id.set(44), posts.title.set('Second')],
    ])
    .returning(posts.projection)
    .all(database);
final insertedById = <int, Post>{
  for (final post in inserted) post.id: post,
};
```

The outer collection and every row must be non-empty. Every row must use the
same table, contain no duplicate columns, and preserve the first row's exact
column objects and order. Odroe validates the complete input before building
SQL. It does not split work automatically; the caller must stay within the
provider's bound-parameter limit.

This is one statement. `atomicWrite` is different: it makes several explicit
statements one atomic unit. SQLite, D1, and PostgreSQL support `RETURNING` on
the multi-row insert; MySQL/MariaDB accepts the write but not `RETURNING`.
Returned row order is database-defined, so correlate by a stable key as above,
never by input index.

Column assignment and predicate types are checked by Dart. Joined reads qualify
columns; left joins require an explicitly optional result selection. Updating
or deleting every row requires the `allRows` confirmation token. Mutations stay
single-table.

Generated statements retain their dialect. SQLite and D1 use
`SqlDialect.sqlite`, PostgreSQL uses `postgres`, and MySQL/MariaDB uses `mysql`.
An explicit mismatch throws `SqlException(SqlErrorCode.unsupported)` before
the driver sends the statement.

`BoundSql.parts` is the escape hatch for source-owned SQL. Fragments are
trusted source and values are bound. Pin `dialect` and `kind` when known; Odroe
does not parse arbitrary SQL to invent portability.

## Ownership follows the host

| Runtime | Resource scope | Odroe module contract |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Flutter + SQLite | Application | `DatabaseModule.owned`, closed with `AppContext` |
| Native server + SQLite/PostgreSQL/MySQL | Process | requests borrow; `Server.onClose` closes |
| Cloudflare D1 | Invocation | request-owned wrapper around the host binding |

A request-scoped module must not close a shared process pool. A Fetch isolate
has no reliable process-shutdown hook, so D1 remains invocation-scoped.

## Provider status is explicit

| Entrypoint | Status | Current boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `database_sqlite.dart` | Verified | Native CRUD, returning rows, batches, transactions, migrations |
| `database_postgres.dart` | Verified | Single connection, bounded pool, transactions |
| `database_mysql.dart` | Preview | MySQL and MariaDB, one serialized connection |
| `database_d1.dart` | Preview | Local Workerd/D1 contract, atomic batches |

All drivers are in the same Dart package but use separate entrypoints so the
wrong implementation is not reachable from a target. Package dependency
resolution and native build hooks still have cost; inspect real artifacts when
binary size matters.

Schema files remain application-owned. Native SQLite and D1 may consume the
same append-only `NNNN_snake_case.sql` history while keeping provider-owned
ledgers. Native verifies the exact SQL string and applies one file atomically before
the listener starts; D1 remains an explicit Wrangler deployment step. This is
not a universal schema abstraction: PostgreSQL and MySQL/MariaDB still use an
application-owned provider path, and Odroe does not generate models, schema
diffs, or down migrations.

Continue with [Database providers](/docs/guides/database-providers) for working
code, pool ownership, D1 bindings, and the complete runtime matrix.
